 {d:3,t:"why",best:1,st:"Containers and global trade",q:"How did the shipping container, introduced in 1956, change the world economy?",o:["It made moving goods so cheap that distant factories became worthwhile","It made shipping slower","It ended trade by sea","It replaced railways"],a:0,why:"Standard boxes move from ship to train to lorry without unpacking."},
 {d:4,t:"mem",st:"Containers and global trade",q:"Why was Shanghai’s Yangshan port built on islands about 30 km offshore?",o:["The Yangtze’s mouth was too shallow for the biggest ships","Land in Shanghai was banned for ports","To avoid typhoons","To be closer to Japan"],a:0,why:"It is joined to the city by a 32 km bridge; Shanghai handles about 50 million standard boxes a year."},
 {d:3,t:"why",st:"The world’s biggest fleet sails under Liberia",q:"Why do so many ships fly a flag of convenience, such as Liberia’s?",o:["Low fees, light rules and cheap crews","Liberia owns most ships","The flag brings good luck","It is required by the UN"],a:0,why:"A ship registers wherever is cheapest, whoever owns it; Liberia’s registry is run from Virginia."}
